# PART 5 — ILLUSTRATION CONTENT, LABELS AND ACCURACY

Every illustration must have an appropriate amount of explanatory information.

Where relevant, include:

- Main title.
- Clearly labelled structures.
- Direction arrows.
- Process stages.
- Scale or orientation.
- Legends.
- Units.
- Scientific terminology.
- Short explanatory annotations.

Every illustration must have:

1. A unique illustration ID.
2. Its assigned topic ID.
3. A topic-specific title.
4. A concise explanatory caption.
5. Alternative text for accessibility.
6. Source or reference attribution where it relies on external factual material.
7. A description of whether it is measured, anatomical, observational, schematic or conceptual.

Do not overload illustrations with paragraphs of text.

Keep detailed explanations in the accompanying topic content.

## Accuracy rules

- Do not invent scientific measurements.
- Do not invent numerical scales.
- Do not invent anatomical structures.
- Do not invent biological stages.
- Do not invent causal relationships.
- Do not make uncertain theories look settled.
- Do not label conceptual models as experimentally measured results.
- Use approximate labels when precision is not supported.
- Preserve uncertainty where relevant.

If available research content does not support a detailed illustration, create a simpler accurate visual rather than inventing details.

